{'callId': 'expect@58', 'startTime': 200962.372, 'method': 'expect', 'params': {'expected': "⋮⋮I. Before the floodI returned to Hollins with my notebook in my coat. I wanted to find out why I remembered the water before I remembered the rain. At the old bridge, Mara Okafor stood beside the gauge. She had tied a blue ribbon around the post to mark the level she remembered from childhood.I walked with Mara Okafor to the center of Hollins, past the theater and the neighbor's empty shop. We argue that memory begins in places like this. I wrote the sentence down, then wondered why I had suddenly borrowed a crowd to say something I believed alone.The shopkeeper offered bread, milk, and apples. At the next door, a volunteer had gathered boots, coats, and blankets. I carried my notebook, a pencil and matches. My coat was grey. The color had faded in the sun, though I remembered its color as blue. I counted three doors, two windows and 4 steps before the lane disappeared into mud.⋮⋮II. The waterlineI followed the wall to Hollis and stopped at the painted line. We believe the river keeps a record. I could see my own reflection below the mark, trembling whenever a truck crossed the bridge. As we have seen, a record is only useful when someone knows how to read it.My family and I worked at the shelter that evening. We all carried boxes from the school to the dry upper rooms. I kept my notes in the pocket nearest my chest. The day had become a list of small decisions: where to put the cups, who needed a blanket, when to go home.⋮⋮III. After the waterIn the morning, Mara Okafor found me beside the bridge. We think the town will remember this differently. I crossed out the borrowed certainty and listened to the sounds around me: the slow scrape of a shovel, water running through a broken gutter, a door that would no longer close.I returned to Hollins a week later. The first dry pavement looked unfamiliar. My notebook was still warped at the corners. I opened it at the last clean page and wrote what I could see, leaving enough room for the things I had missed.A witness said, “We argue that the river forgets nothing.”"}}
